📄 Product Requirements Document (PRD)
Project Name

High-Throughput Event Ingestion & Analytics Platform

1. Overview
   1.1 Problem Statement

Modern applications generate a large volume of user interaction and system events (page views, clicks, lifecycle events).
Handling these events synchronously causes:

Increased API latency

Database overload

Poor scalability under traffic spikes

There is a need for a high-throughput, low-latency ingestion system that decouples event intake from processing and provides near real-time aggregated analytics.

1.2 Goal

Build a scalable event ingestion and aggregation platform that:

Accepts events at very high rates

Keeps ingestion latency consistently low

Processes and aggregates data asynchronously

Exposes aggregated data via read-optimized APIs

Visualizes system health and analytics via a lightweight dashboard

1.3 Non-Goals

This project does not aim to:

Provide millisecond-accurate real-time analytics

Guarantee exactly-once event delivery

Support global, multi-region replication

Replace production analytics products (GA, Mixpanel)

2. Target Users
   Primary Users

Backend / Platform Engineers

Engineering teams generating telemetry or analytics events

Secondary Users

Engineering Managers

Developers validating system performance and correctness

3. Event Context & Input Model
   3.1 Context

Events represent observability and analytics signals, not business transactions.

Event sources may include:

Web applications

Mobile apps

Backend services

Internal systems

Events are:

Append-only

High volume

Loss-tolerant (within reason)

Not directly tied to business correctness

3.2 Event Types (Examples)
Category Examples
User Interaction page_view, button_click
Funnel Events checkout_started, signup_started
System Events job_failed, email_sent

Event types are controlled values, not free text.

3.3 Event Data Model

Each event must contain:

Field Type Description
eventType string Type of event
timestamp number Client-side epoch timestamp (ms)
source string Origin (web, mobile, backend)
entityId string (optional) User or session identifier
context object (optional) Lightweight metadata
Example Payload
{
"eventType": "page_view",
"timestamp": 1736947200000,
"source": "web",
"entityId": "user_123",
"context": {
"page": "/pricing",
"referrer": "/home"
}
}

4. Functional Requirements (Backend)
   4.1 Event Ingestion API

Endpoint

POST /events

Requirements

Validate basic schema only

Enqueue event for async processing

Respond immediately

Must not access database synchronously

Constraints

Ingestion path must remain lightweight

Target p95 latency < 20ms

4.2 Asynchronous Processing

Requirements

Events must be processed via background workers

Workers consume events in batches

Processing must be retryable and fault-tolerant

Responsibilities

Group events by type and time bucket

Increment counters in fast storage

Avoid per-event DB writes

4.3 Aggregation Strategy

Time Buckets

Per minute

Per hour

Per day

Aggregation Dimensions

eventType

time bucket

source (optional)

Aggregation is eventually consistent, not real-time.

4.4 Durable Storage

Requirements

Aggregated data is persisted in PostgreSQL

Writes are batched

Schema optimized for read-heavy workloads

No raw event storage required for MVP

4.5 Analytics Read APIs

Expose read-only endpoints for aggregated data.

Examples

GET /analytics/events?eventType=page_view&period=hour
GET /analytics/events?period=day

Constraints

Predictable query shapes

Indexed access only

No heavy computation at request time

5. Load Simulation & Testing
   5.1 Load Generator

A dedicated load generator will:

Simulate realistic event distributions

Generate configurable event rates

Mimic burst and sustained traffic

Measure request latency and success rate

5.2 Traffic Characteristics

Skewed distribution (hot events)

Burst traffic patterns

Sustained high throughput

6. Frontend Requirements (Dashboard)
   6.1 Frontend Purpose

The frontend serves as an observability dashboard, not a user-facing product.

It exists to:

Visualize system behavior under load

Validate aggregation correctness

Demonstrate scalability and performance characteristics

6.2 Frontend Scope
Explicitly Included

Read-only dashboards

Polling-based data refresh

System health visualization

Explicitly Excluded

Authentication

Real-time streaming via WebSockets

User-level analytics

Complex UI interactions

6.3 Dashboard Pages
Page 1: System Overview

Purpose
Show system health and ingestion performance.

Metrics Displayed

Events ingested per second

Queue depth

Worker processing rate

Aggregation lag (seconds)

Page 2: Event Analytics

Purpose
Show correctness of aggregated data.

Data Displayed

Event counts by type

Time-series graphs (minute / hour)

Source breakdown

Data refreshes via polling (5–10s interval).

Page 3: Load Control (Optional)

Purpose
Control and observe load tests.

Controls

Start / stop load generator

Events per second

Event distribution ratios

6.4 Frontend Tech Constraints

Built with Next.js + Tailwind

Uses read-only APIs

Polling over push mechanisms

Minimal client-side state

7. Non-Functional Requirements
   7.1 Performance Targets
   Metric Target
   Sustained throughput 5,000–10,000 events/sec
   Ingestion p95 latency < 20 ms
   Aggregation delay ≤ 60 seconds
   7.2 Scalability

Horizontal worker scaling supported

Read and write paths decoupled

No code changes required to increase throughput

7.3 Reliability & Fault Tolerance

Ingestion remains available if workers or DB slow down

Queue retries for transient failures

At-least-once processing semantics acceptable

7.4 Observability

The system must expose:

Ingestion rate

Queue backlog

Worker lag

Aggregation delay

Error rates

8. Success Criteria (Definition of Done)

The project is considered complete when:

Sustains 5k–10k events/sec on a single node

Ingestion latency stays below 20ms p95

Aggregated data is eventually correct

System remains stable under sustained load

Bottlenecks and limits are documented clearly

9. Risks & Trade-offs
   Area Decision Trade-off
   Consistency Eventual Faster ingestion
   Delivery At-least-once Possible duplicates
   Real-time Near real-time Higher throughput
   Storage Aggregates only Less raw data

10. Key Takeaway

This project prioritizes:
Throughput over immediacy
Stability over strict consistency
Learning system behavior under load
It is designed to demonstrate scalability, performance tuning, and async system design, not UI polish.

